From a47d900d43349b5accaba5bb661628109cd1631f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yzddmr6 <46088090+yzddmr6@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 21:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] libssc: choose the stream delivery by sensor type

With wake-up delivery, which libssc requests for every stream, the SSC
signals the application processor for each sample it delivers. On the
OnePlus Pad 2 Pro (SM8750) the 5 Hz accelerometer stream of
iio-sensor-proxy therefore raises about five ADSP SMP2P interrupts a
second. Selecting non-wakeup delivery through SSC_SUSPEND_MODE (previous
patch) removed all of them while the samples kept flowing at the same
rate: the interrupts are the wake-up signal of the delivery type, not
something the firmware does for every sample.

Wake-up delivery is only right for sensors whose events have to wake the
system. Android separates the two: a non-wake-up sensor neither keeps the
SoC awake nor wakes it to report, and before the per-sensor flag only
proximity and significant motion were wake-up sensors by type; the
stock sensor HAL of this tablet chooses the delivery for each sensor. Do
the same for the data types libssc knows:

  accel, gyro, mag, rotv    no-wakeup (delivery_type 1)
  every other data type     wake-up (delivery_type 0), as before

Only the request that starts a stream carries the per-type delivery;
discovery, attribute and disable requests stay wake-up. The light sensor
keeps wake-up delivery: it reports on change rather than per sample, and
changing it is left until it has been measured.

Streams are still not batched by default. Non-wakeup delivery alone
already removes the interrupts while the processor is awake, and a batch
period delays each sample by up to one period; the orientation of the
screen is taken from these samples, so batching would only add latency.

SSC_SUSPEND_MODE=wakeup|nowakeup and SSC_BATCH_PERIOD_US /
SSC_FLUSH_PERIOD_US keep their meaning and still apply to every stream
of the process, so SSC_SUSPEND_MODE=wakeup restores the requests of
libssc 0.4.4 for A/B measurements or as a fallback. Unset or empty, the
delivery follows the sensor type. Every stream request now logs the
delivery it asks for and whether the sensor type or SSC_SUSPEND_MODE
chose it.

The sensor passes its data type with the stream request through a new
ssc_client_send_for_sensor(); ssc_client_send() is unchanged for the
requests that are not about one sensor type. The unit test checks the
default of each type, the requests of each type with and without the
overrides, and the wire bytes of a wake-up and a non-wakeup request.

What non-wakeup delivery does while the application processor is
suspended (events held or dropped by the SSC, and delivered when it
resumes) has not been measured.

